1Q. Optimized Linux File System Layout for a 50 GB Server

The main objectives are to separate frequently growing data, prevent logs from consuming the entire disk, and ensure critical services continue running even when one area becomes full.

Proposed partition layout
Partition                  	Size	        Purpose
/boot	                      1 GB	        Kernel and boot files
/	                          10 GB	        Operating system and applications
/var	                      8 GB        	Variable data and package/service files
/var/log	                  10 GB	        System and application logs
/home	                      10 GB	        User files
/tmp	                      3 GB	        Temporary files
Swap	                      4 GB	        Virtual memory
Free/LVM space	            4 GB        	Future expansion
Total = 50 GB

A separate /var/log is particularly important. If logs fill their partition, the root filesystem and critical system files remain protected.

Storage management

Using LVM (Logical Volume Manager) is preferable because logical volumes can be resized more easily than fixed partitions.

For example:

pvcreate /dev/sdb
vgcreate vgserver /dev/sdb
lvcreate -L 10G -n lv_log vgserver
mkfs.ext4 /dev/vgserver/lv_log

Mount it as /var/log through /etc/fstab.

Log rotation

logrotate should be configured to keep logs below 10 GB.

Example:

/var/log/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 7
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
    size 500M
}

This rotates logs daily and also rotates them when they reach the specified size. Old logs are compressed to save space.

Check the configuration using:

logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf
Monitoring disk usage
df -h
du -sh /var/log/*

Automated monitoring can trigger alerts when usage reaches, for example, 80–85%.

Ensuring service continuity

Critical services should not depend on a filesystem that can be filled by logs. Separate /var/log protects /, while log rotation and compression prevent uncontrolled growth.

Conclusion: A combination of separate filesystems + LVM + logrotate + disk monitoring provides efficient storage utilization, keeps logs below the 10 GB limit, and prevents disk exhaustion from interrupting critical services.

2Q. Kernel-Level Optimization for High CPU Workloads

Frequent context switching causes CPU time to be wasted saving and restoring process state instead of performing useful work. Since hardware cannot be upgraded, kernel and scheduler tuning should be used.

1. Analyze context switching

Commands such as:

vmstat 1

and:

pidstat -w 1

can identify excessive context switching.

vmstat reports system-wide context switches, while pidstat can identify processes producing large numbers of switches.

2. CPU affinity

A process can be restricted to selected CPUs:

taskset -cp 2,3 <PID>

This reduces unnecessary migration of processes between CPUs and can improve cache locality.

3. Scheduler priority

Normal processes can be given lower or higher scheduling priority:

nice -n 10 ./application

or:

renice -n 5 -p <PID>

For real-time workloads, Linux provides real-time scheduling policies such as SCHED_FIFO and SCHED_RR.

For example:

chrt -f 50 ./realtime_app

However, real-time priorities must be used carefully because a CPU-intensive real-time process can starve ordinary system processes.

4. Kernel preemption

A kernel configured with an appropriate preemption model can reduce scheduling latency. For real-time workloads, a PREEMPT_RT kernel can provide significantly more deterministic scheduling behavior.

5. Kernel modules

Unnecessary kernel modules and drivers can be disabled where appropriate to reduce unnecessary kernel activity.

Check loaded modules:

lsmod

A module can be removed when it is not required:

sudo modprobe -r module_name

Only unused modules should be removed because removing a required driver can disrupt hardware or services.

6. Interrupt optimization

High interrupt rates can also contribute to CPU overhead. Tools such as:

cat /proc/interrupts

help identify interrupt-heavy devices.

irqbalance can distribute hardware interrupts across CPUs where appropriate.

7. Measure after every change

Performance should be measured before and after tuning using:

top
vmstat 1
pidstat -w 1
mpstat -P ALL 1

Conclusion: The best approach is to combine CPU affinity, scheduler priorities, appropriate preemption configuration, interrupt balancing, and removal of unnecessary modules. Changes should be measured carefully because aggressive real-time scheduling can reduce overall system responsiveness.

3Q. Processing 1 Million Log Entries per Day Using Shell Scripting

Processing one million lines within two minutes requires avoiding inefficient commands and unnecessary process creation.

The main principle is to process the file in a single streaming pass rather than repeatedly using grep, cut, sed, and sort.

Efficient approach using awk

Suppose the log file is:

2026-08-25 10:00:01 ERROR user123 ...
2026-08-25 10:00:02 INFO user456 ...

A single awk process can filter and count entries:

awk '
/ERROR/ { error++ }
/WARN/  { warn++ }
/INFO/  { info++ }
END {
    print "ERROR:", error
    print "WARN:", warn
    print "INFO:", info
}' application.log

This makes only one sequential pass through the file.

Why awk?

awk is efficient because:

It processes input as a stream.
It avoids creating a new process for every line.
It combines filtering, field extraction and calculations.
It performs well on large text files.
Avoid inefficient commands

An inefficient design might be:

grep ERROR log | cut ... | sort | uniq ...

repeated many times.

Each command creates additional processing stages and requires additional scans of the data.

Instead, combine operations:

awk -F' ' '$3 == "ERROR" { count++ } END { print count }' log
Parallel processing

If the file is extremely large and the workload can safely be divided, it can be split into chunks:

split -n l/4 application.log chunk_

The chunks can then be processed in parallel:

for f in chunk_*; do
    awk '/ERROR/ {e++} END {print e+0}' "$f" &
done
wait

However, parallelism should only be used when the available CPU resources justify it. Excessive parallel processes can actually make performance worse.

Additional optimizations

Use:

LC_ALL=C

for byte-oriented processing where appropriate, because locale processing can add overhead.

For compressed logs, process them directly where possible:

zcat application.log.gz | awk '/ERROR/ {count++} END {print count}'
Performance measurement
time ./process_logs.sh

For one million entries, the goal is to keep the implementation to a small number of sequential passes and avoid unnecessary external commands.

Conclusion: A streaming awk-based single-pass solution is the preferred shell approach. It minimizes process creation, disk I/O, and repeated scanning, making completion within the two-minute target much more achievable.

4Q. Managing a Faulty Process Consuming 90% CPU

The system should not be restarted because critical services must remain available. The process should therefore be identified, monitored, and controlled carefully.

Step 1: Identify the process

Use:

top

or:

ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head

These commands identify processes consuming the most CPU.

Suppose the faulty process has PID 2450.

Step 2: Investigate the process
ps -p 2450 -o pid,ppid,user,%cpu,%mem,stat,cmd

Check:

PID
Parent PID
Owner
CPU usage
Memory usage
Process state
Command

Also inspect open files:

lsof -p 2450
Step 3: Monitor continuously
top -p 2450

or:

pidstat -p 2450 1

This determines whether CPU consumption is continuously high or only temporary.

Step 4: Reduce its priority

If the process must remain alive but should receive fewer CPU resources:

renice +10 -p 2450

This allows other important processes to receive more CPU time.

Step 5: Restrict CPU usage

If appropriate, use CPU affinity:

taskset -cp 0 2450

This restricts the process to CPU 0.

Another option is systemd resource control for services, using controls such as CPU quotas.

Step 6: Stop gracefully if necessary

First send a normal termination signal:

kill -TERM 2450

Wait and verify:

ps -p 2450

Only if the process does not respond should a forced kill be considered:

kill -KILL 2450

SIGKILL should be a last resort because the process cannot clean up resources.

Step 7: Check critical services

After intervention:

systemctl --failed
systemctl status critical-service

Monitor CPU and system load using:

uptime
vmstat 1

Conclusion: The safest strategy is identify → monitor → lower priority or constrain CPU → gracefully terminate if necessary → verify critical services. This solves the CPU problem without rebooting the server or unnecessarily affecting critical services.

5Q. RAM Allocation for 5 VMs on a 16 GB Host
Given
Total physical RAM = 16 GB
Number of VMs = 5
Minimum RAM per VM = 2 GB
Host OS requirement = 4 GB

Minimum required memory:

5×2+4=14 GB

Therefore:

16−14=2 GB

Only 2 GB remains available after satisfying the minimum requirements.

Proposed allocation
Resource	RAM
Host OS	4 GB
VM1	2.4 GB
VM2	2.4 GB
VM3	2.4 GB
VM4	2.4 GB
VM5	2.4 GB
Total	16 GB

This gives every VM more than its required 2 GB while keeping the host at its required 4 GB.

However, allocating all 16 GB leaves no safety margin for host-level bursts, virtualization overhead, or cache requirements. Therefore, in a real deployment, the exact VM allocations should be adjusted according to actual workload.

Ballooning

Memory ballooning allows a hypervisor to reclaim unused memory from VMs and give it to other VMs when necessary.

For example, if VM1 is using only 1 GB actively while VM2 needs additional memory, unused memory can potentially be reclaimed from VM1.

Memory overcommit

The hypervisor can use controlled memory overcommit, but this should be used carefully. Overcommitting memory too aggressively can cause swapping and significantly reduce performance.

Swap

The host can maintain a small swap area as an emergency mechanism, but swap should not be treated as a replacement for RAM. Heavy swapping can severely reduce VM performance.

Prioritization

If some VMs run more important applications, they should receive higher resource priority. Less important VMs can have lower memory/CPU shares.

Recommended strategy

A practical allocation would be:

Host OS: 4 GB
Each VM: 2 GB minimum
Remaining 2 GB: used through dynamic allocation/ballooning, workload-based allocation, and controlled overhead management.

If all five VMs genuinely require more than 2 GB simultaneously, the 16 GB physical limit becomes the bottleneck; virtualization cannot create additional physical RAM.

Conclusion: The mathematical minimum is 14 GB, leaving only 2 GB of flexibility. Dynamic memory/ballooning and workload prioritization can improve utilization, but sustained workloads requiring more memory than the physical server provides will require additional RAM or moving some VMs to another host.
